  "id": "DrugBank_DB00694 - MONDO_18435",
  "verbalization": "Daunorubicin is associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a shared pathway involving Glasdegib, suggesting a potential role in AML pathogenesis. The evidence indicates that Daunorubicin is indicated for both Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation and Without Maturation, both of which are linked to Glasdegib. Glasdegib, in turn, is indicated for AML with specific chromosomal abnormalities, including Inv3(p21;q26.2) or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2). This suggests that Daunorubicin may exert its effects through a mechanism that is broadly applicable to AML, possibly by targeting pathways that are common across different subtypes of the disease. The presence of a shared indication for Glasdegib across different AML subtypes implies that the therapeutic relationship between Daunorubicin and AML may be mediated through a common downstream effect, such as the inhibition of specific molecular pathways. The evidence supports a potential role for Daunorubicin in AML, particularly in cases where Glasdegib is involved, though further studies are needed to clarify the exact mechanism of action and its relevance to the specific genetic profile of Inv3(p21;q26.2) or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2).",
